{
 "dataset": "Kaggle 'Resume Dataset' by Snehaan Bhawal (snehaanbhawal/resume-dataset), 2,484 resume examples scraped from livecareer.com",
 "licence": "CC0: Public Domain, as stated on the Kaggle dataset page (checked 2026-11-09). The scraped content's own provenance is livecareer.com example resumes; no names are present. No resume text is stored in this repo: only the dataset IDs and the labelled skills.",
 "sampling": "1 resume per job category (24) plus 1 extra IT resume, chosen with pandas groupby().sample(1, random_state=11); PDFs from the dataset's data/<CATEGORY>/<ID>.pdf are what the extractor reads.",
 "labelled_by": "A reviewer reading each resume (here: Claude, in a Claude Code session, on 2026-11-09). NOT a team of independent humans, so please spot-check a few before quoting the numbers as ground truth.",
 "protocol": [
  "Gold = vocabulary skills (models/skill_vocab.json, after configs/skill_aliases.yaml) that the resume claims as a skill, tool, technology, certification-skill or domain competency, in a skills/highlights list or used as a capability in experience.",
  "Not gold: job titles, employers, degree/institution names, generic nouns in running prose (customer, system, team, business, support, research, reports...) unless the resume lists them as skills.",
  "Soft skills count only when explicitly listed.",
  "Skills the model cannot express (not in the 6,000-entry vocabulary) are ignored: they are not counted as misses.",
  "Spelling variants a human would accept count as present (e.g. 'Power Point' = powerpoint, 'Bi-lingual' = bilingual, 'lead generating' = lead generation), even when the extractor cannot yet match them.",
  "Labels were made from the resume text plus an ungated list of every vocabulary phrase found in it (a candidate pool), so recall is measured against what is expressible in the vocabulary."
 ],
